course: arts
lesson: 43
title: Olympiad: film, stage and design puzzles ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This last olympiad block mixes film, animation, theatre, design, buildings and building bricks into puzzles: counting frames and photos, planning a shoot, reading theatre slang, and spotting why a shape stands or wobbles.
explain: Frame puzzles are simple multiplying: the cinema traditionally shows 24 still pictures, called frames, every second, so a 3-second shot needs 3 lots of 24, which is 72 frames.
explain: Planning puzzles hide a trap in the order: films are usually shot out of order, all the scenes in one place together, and the editor joins them in the story's order afterwards.
explain: Picture a stage manager in a split costume five minutes before curtain-up saying, "Bit of a pickle." That is understatement: the problem is real, and the safety pins and tape come out fast.
explain: People say it their own way: in South Africa a "robot" is a traffic light, in Canada a "toque" is a woolly hat, in the West Country "proper job" means well done, and "sus" means suspicious.
fact: A film is many still pictures called frames, traditionally shown at 24 every second in the cinema; cartoons often use 12 to 24 drawings for each second.
fact: Stop-motion means moving models a tiny bit, taking a photo and repeating; Wallace and Gromit are made this way with plasticine by Aardman.
fact: Silent films came before about 1927; King Kong, with its small gorilla model moved by hand, came out in 1933; Toy Story, the first full-length computer-animated film, in 1995; Shrek in 2001.
fact: Films are usually shot out of order, for example all the scenes in one place together, and the editor puts the shots in order afterwards.
fact: A clapperboard shows the scene and take number; a take is one try at filming a shot.
fact: In front of a green screen, the computer later replaces everything green with a new background.
fact: Foley artists make everyday sounds in a studio and match them to the film, famously using halves of coconut shells for horses' hooves.
fact: Theatre slang: "break a leg" means good luck, corpsing means laughing on stage when you should not, and cheating out means standing a little open to the audience.
fact: A square frame can squash sideways, but a triangle cannot change shape without breaking a side, so a diagonal brace makes a square strong.
fact: LEGO was founded in Denmark in 1932, and since 1958 its bricks have used the same stud-and-tube fit; bricks that overlap the joins make a stronger wall.
fact: The Nike swoosh was designed in 1971 by Carolyn Davidson, who was reportedly paid 35 dollars; it became valuable because of the brand built around it.
fact: The Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World's Fair; many artists called it ugly, and it was meant to stand only 20 years.
exercise: A shot lasts 3 seconds in the cinema. How many frames is that? | 72 | 27 | 48 | 8
exercise: An animator takes 12 photos for each second of stop-motion. How many photos for a 10-second scene? | 120 | 22 | 12 | 1,200
exercise: A cartoon uses 12 drawings each second. How long does a stack of 48 drawings last? | 4 seconds | 36 seconds | 2 seconds | 60 seconds
exercise: Put in order, oldest first: Shrek, King Kong, Toy Story. | King Kong, Toy Story, Shrek | Toy Story, King Kong, Shrek | King Kong, Shrek, Toy Story | Shrek, Toy Story, King Kong
exercise: Was King Kong of 1933 a silent film? | No, it came after talking films began around 1927 | Yes, all black-and-white films were silent ones | Yes, because talking films only began in 1995 | No, because it was the first film ever made
exercise: The script goes: scene 1 kitchen, 2 park, 3 kitchen, 4 park, 5 kitchen. What is the smartest shooting plan? | Scenes 1, 3, 5 in the kitchen, then 2 and 4 in the park | Scenes 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in order, moving the crew each time | Scene 5 first, then 4, 3, 2 and 1, going backwards | Scenes 1 and 2 on day one, then the rest a week later
exercise: The clapperboard says "Scene 12, Take 7". What does the 7 tell the editor? | It is the seventh try at that shot | It is the film's seventh scene | Seven actors are in this shot | The shot lasts seven seconds
exercise: The Eiffel Tower was built for 1889 and meant to stand only 20 years. In which year should it have come down? | 1909 | 1989 | 1869 | 1920
exercise: How many years after LEGO was founded did today's stud-and-tube fit arrive? | 26 | 58 | 32 | 16
exercise: Which bookshelf frame will stop wobbling sideways? | A square with one diagonal brace | A square made from thicker sticks | A taller square with longer sides | A square with a smaller square inside it
exercise: Which tower of bricks is strongest? | Bricks overlapping the joins, like a real wall | Bricks stacked straight up in neat columns | Only the tallest bricks, one on another | Bricks with no studs, so nothing holds them
exercise: The swoosh cost about 35 dollars in 1971 and is now worth a fortune. Why? | The brand built around it | The ink it was drawn with | The price of the paper used | The size of the first drawing
exercise: Odd one out: corpsing, cheating out, break a leg, bombing. Which is NOT theatre talk? | Bombing | Corpsing | Cheating out | Break a leg
exercise: Horse hooves clop in a film, but there was no horse in the studio. Who most likely made the sound? | A Foley artist | The film's editor | A stunt performer | The voice actor
exercise: An actor in front of a green screen wears a bright green T-shirt. What will happen? | Her shirt vanishes and the background shows through | Nothing, the computer only changes the wall behind her | Her shirt turns bright red in the finished film | The camera refuses to film her until she changes
exercise: Wallace and Gromit are moved a tiny bit and photographed again and again. What is this called? | Stop-motion | Dubbing | Foley | Green screen
test: "fam, 24 drawings a sec, took a million years to draw. how many for 5 seconds" | 120 | 1,000,000 | 29 | 24
test: "12 photos a second, ok so 6 seconds, no wait, 8 seconds of clay dog. how many photos" | 96 | 72 | 20 | 18
test: Ruby says, "They filmed the ending first? That's sus" ("sus" means suspicious). What is the truth? | Shooting out of order is normal and saves time | Films must be shot from start to finish | The ending was filmed first by mistake | They had lost the first half of the script
test: A mate says, "That stunt fall's nothing, I'll do it off the garage roof, easy." What is the wise answer? | No: stunts are done by trained professionals with hidden safety gear | Yes: if it looks easy in a film, it is easy at home too | Yes: but only with a pillow on the ground underneath | No: only because the garage roof might get damaged
test: "The punch totally connected, no cap!" ("no cap" means honestly). What really happened? | It missed; the angle, sound and reaction sold it | The actor really was hit, but was paid extra for it | The punch hit a hidden pillow under the actor's shirt | The film was speeded up so nobody could feel the hit
test: Five minutes before curtain-up the costume splits and the stage manager says, "Bit of a pickle." What is she really saying? | It's a real problem, so grab the pins and tape fast | Nothing is wrong, she is just hungry for pickles | The show must be cancelled at once | The actor must just go on stage with the costume split open
test: A kid on a market stall sells a "Nikey" hoodie with a wonky swoosh. What is it? | A knock-off copying the real brand's look | The real brand, sold cheaper on market stalls | A new brand that the real Nike company owns | A legal copy, because one letter has changed
test: A café puts its logo of 50 tiny stars on a pencil, and it looks like a smudge. What did the designer forget? | To test the logo at a tiny size | To use even more, smaller stars on it | To make the pencil much longer | To print it in more colours
test: Dad, barefoot: "Trod on a LEGO brick, literally died." What really happened, and why? | It hurt a lot: his weight pressed on one tiny hard spot | He really died and then came back to life | It didn't hurt, he was joking | The brick was as sharp as a knife and cut his foot deeply
test: A flipbook's pages show a ball high, then lower, then lower, then on the ground. What do you see if you flip it from the back to the front? | The ball rises up from the ground | The ball falls down to the ground | The ball stays still on the ground | The ball rolls sideways along the ground
test: In 1889 many artists called the Eiffel Tower ugly; now millions visit it. What does that show? | Taste in buildings can change | The artists were paid to hate it | It was rebuilt to look prettier | Nobody visits ugly buildings
test: A Bristol director says "Proper job!" ("proper job" means well done) after take 9. Did the actors act badly in takes 1 to 8? | Not necessarily: a plane, a wobble or the light can mean another take | Yes: every new take means the actors got it wrong again | Yes: a good actor always gets it right on the very first take | No: the director just likes the number nine more than the others
exam: Determine the number of frames contained in a five-second shot projected at twenty-four frames per second. | One hundred and twenty | Twenty-nine | Forty-eight | Seven hundred and twenty
exam: Identify the principal reason for which films are commonly shot out of the order of the story. | To film all scenes at one location together and save time and money | To confuse the actors so that their performances seem more natural | Because the law requires that endings be filmed before beginnings | Because editors are unable to join shots in the order of the story
exam: Ascertain why a diagonal brace renders a square frame rigid. | It divides the square into triangles, which cannot change shape | It makes the square heavier, so it can no longer move at all | It makes the square taller and therefore much stronger | It glues the corners together so that they cannot turn
exam: Select the films in correct chronological order, the earliest first. | King Kong, Toy Story, Shrek | Toy Story, King Kong, Shrek | Shrek, King Kong, Toy Story | King Kong, Shrek, Toy Story
